---
import EnglishBreadcrumb from '../../../../components/english/EnglishBreadcrumb.astro'
import { PipelineRecoveryLab } from '../../../../components/english/PipelineRecoveryLab'
import EnglishArticleLayout from '../../../../layouts/EnglishArticleLayout.astro'
import { buildRecoveryReference } from '../../../../features/english-recovery/model'
import recoveryStylesUrl from '../../../../styles/layouts/english-pipeline-recovery.css?url'
import { getEnglishRoute } from '../../../../utils/english-routes'

const title = 'Data Pipeline Failure Recovery: Impact & Safe Rerun | sql.sb'
const description =
  'Bad rows already reached the warehouse while downstream jobs keep running: compute the impact closure, stop safely, replay in dependency order and re-notify consumers.'

const reference = buildRecoveryReference()
---

<EnglishArticleLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  active="warehouse"
  styleUrls={[recoveryStylesUrl]}
>
  <EnglishBreadcrumb
    items={[
      { label: 'English home', key: 'overview' },
      { label: 'Data warehouse', key: 'warehouse' },
      { label: 'Pipeline failure recovery' },
    ]}
  />

  <header class="english-article__header">
    <p class="english-article__eyebrow">Data warehouse · pipelines</p>
    <h1 class="english-article__title">
      Data pipeline failure recovery: stop, replay and re-notify
    </h1>
    <p class="english-article__lede">
      When wrong data has already moved downstream, “rerun the failed task” is not a recovery plan.
      The finished jobs are still marked <code>success</code>, some jobs are still running, and
      consumers may already be showing the bad number.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="english-answer">
    <span class="english-answer__label">Quick answer</span>
    <p>
      Recovery answers four questions in order: which task instances are affected (SQL lineage plus
      declared scheduler dependencies, for the same business date); which of them are still running
      and must stop or be blocked first; in what order the affected instances are recomputed after
      the corrected source passes its checks; and which consumers already loaded the old result and
      still need a reload and a confirmation.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="english-article__body">
    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="scenario">
      <h2 id="scenario">The hard part starts after the bad rows land</h2>
      <p>
        A load for business date <code>2026-10-16</code> registers 12,480 rows in the arrival
        control table, but the partition holds 11,904 — 576 transactions never arrived, and one
        arrived with the wrong amount (<code>400.00</code> instead of <code>480.00</code>). The
        partition is not empty and the task status is <code>success</code>, so nothing in the run
        history looks broken.
      </p>
      <p>
        By the time the correction is prepared, the DWD transaction detail has already succeeded on
        the bad rows, the merchant aggregate is still running, and the ADS report plus two consumers
        have not started yet. The lab below reproduces exactly this incident with a deterministic
        banking fixture: the same input always produces the same closure, the same stop set and the
        same replay order.
      </p>
      <PipelineRecoveryLab client:load />
      <p>
        The lab is deterministic. The reference tables below are generated from the same fixture at
        build time, so every claim on this page can be checked without clicking anything.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="steps">
      <h2 id="steps">Seven recovery states, each with observable evidence</h2>
      <div class="english-table-scroll">
        <table class="english-table">
          <caption>Recovery states and the evidence that releases each one</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">State</th>
              <th scope="col">Question</th>
              <th scope="col">Evidence you need</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>
                <code>BAD_INPUT</code>
              </td>
              <td>Is the source wrong, incomplete, or both?</td>
              <td>Control-table row count vs partition content; a concrete bad row</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                <code>IMPACT_DISCOVERY</code>
              </td>
              <td>Which instances are actually affected?</td>
              <td>Closure over SQL lineage and scheduler dependencies for one business date</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                <code>QUIESCE_RUNNING</code>
              </td>
              <td>Who must stop before anything is replayed?</td>
              <td>
                Running or ready instances inside the closure, plus their side-effect disposition
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                <code>SAFE_RERUN_PLAN</code>
              </td>
              <td>Is it allowed to replay yet?</td>
              <td>Every corrected-source check passes; no normal-schedule writer conflicts</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                <code>ORDERED_REPLAY</code>
              </td>
              <td>What runs before what?</td>
              <td>
                Topological order over dependencies and lineage; each converged branch runs once
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                <code>VALIDATION</code>
              </td>
              <td>Did the recomputed instances produce the corrected result?</td>
              <td>Per-instance success for the incident business date only</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                <code>CONSUMER_NOTIFICATION</code>
              </td>
              <td>Is anyone still showing the old number?</td>
              <td>Consumer evidence: loaded old result, loaded new result, confirmed</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="impact">
      <h2 id="impact">Impact discovery: follow two edge types</h2>
      <p>
        A task list is not an impact list. Starting from the polluted partition, the closure follows
        two independent edge types:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>SQL lineage</strong> — the task reads a table this instance wrote.{' '}
          <code>LEFT JOIN</code> still counts: even when a missing merchant does not change the
          aggregate, the instance is in the lineage and must be recomputed.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Scheduler dependencies</strong> — the downstream job declares{' '}
          <code>depends_on</code> on the upstream task, even without a direct SQL read. The
          completeness check is affected this way.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="english-table-scroll">
        <table class="english-table">
          <caption>Closure for ods_transaction · business date{reference.businessDate}</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Group</th>
              <th scope="col">Count</th>
              <th scope="col">Instances</th>
              <th scope="col">What it means</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr data-tone="wrong">
              <td>Must stop or block first</td>
              <td>{reference.stop.length}</td>
              <td>{reference.stop.map((item) => item.label).join(', ')}</td>
              <td>The instance is still running and would write a result derived from bad input</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Recompute after validation</td>
              <td>{reference.redo.length}</td>
              <td>{reference.redo.map((item) => item.label).join(', ')}</td>
              <td>Already executed, so the published result for this business date is invalid</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Waiting for dependencies</td>
              <td>{reference.waiting.length}</td>
              <td>{reference.waiting.map((item) => item.label).join(', ')}</td>
              <td>Never started; they must not be claimed as “already rerun”</td>
            </tr>
            <tr data-tone="right">
              <td>Untouched</td>
              <td>everything else</td>
              <td>{reference.untouchedTaskIds.join(', ')}, business date 2026-10-15</td>
              <td>Same pipeline, different source or partition — stop nothing here</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Partition scope matters. The instance for <code>2026-10-15</code> is not affected by a
        correction to <code>2026-10-16</code>, even though both instances belong to the same task.
        Impact analysis works at the instance level: task + business date, not task name.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="stop">
      <h2 id="stop">Quiesce: stop running work before replaying</h2>
      <p>
        The merchant aggregate is still running and will read the corrupted DWD result. Replaying
        upstream while it runs would let two writers touch the same table, and the last commit would
        win. The instance goes through <code>running → stopping → stopped → waiting</code>, and the
        stop records what happened to external side effects: no partial file made visible, no push
        that needs recalling.
      </p>
      <p>
        Instances that are merely <code>ready</code> can be blocked before their first execution.
        Instances that never started are left waiting. Unaffected branches are not stopped — that is
        the visible difference between a targeted recovery and a full rebuild.
      </p>
      <div class="english-callout" data-tone="info">
        <strong>A stopped task is not a recovered task</strong>
        <p>
          After quiesce, the closure holds no running instances, but nothing has been recomputed
          yet. Recovery proceeds only when the corrected source passes its checks.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="gate">
      <h2 id="gate">Validate the source before releasing the replay</h2>
      <p>
        Replaying against an incomplete correction produces the same incident twice and destroys the
        log trail that would show it. The gate is explicit evidence, not a successful manual spot
        check:
      </p>
      <div class="english-table-scroll">
        <table class="english-table">
          <caption>Corrected-source checks for{reference.businessDate}</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Check</th>
              <th scope="col">Why it blocks replay</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {reference.odsChecks.map((check) => (
              <tr data-tone={check.passed ? 'right' : 'wrong'}>
                <td>{check.label}</td>
                <td>{check.detail}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        In the lab, the row-count check fails until the partition is corrected. While any check
        fails, the replay plan stays <code>blocked</code>: there is no partial release, and the
        consumers are not notified to reload a result that is about to change again.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="replay">
      <h2 id="replay">Replay in dependency order, once per branch</h2>
      <p>
        The replay plan is a topological order over declared dependencies and SQL lineage. Two DWS
        branches feed the same ADS report; the report runs once, after both branches have been
        recomputed. Nothing runs twice and no downstream instance starts before its upstream
        instance has finished.
      </p>
      <div class="english-table-scroll">
        <table class="english-table">
          <caption>Ordered replay plan for{reference.businessDate}</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">#</th>
              <th scope="col">Instance</th>
              <th scope="col">Action</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {reference.replayOrder.map((item) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{item.order}</td>
                <td>{item.label}</td>
                <td>{item.actionLabel}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The plan is isolated from the normal schedule: it addresses the incident business date, and
        the plan identifier is a lab construct, not a production batch number. The instances that
        were blocked or waiting become runnable only inside this plan.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="verify">
      <h2 id="verify">Verify the result, and expect a second correction</h2>
      <p>
        After replay, every affected instance for <code>2026-10-16</code> ends in{' '}
        <code>success</code>. That statement is scoped: other business dates were not recomputed,
        and <code>success</code> says the job finished, not that the data is correct or that a
        consumer has seen it.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the “corrected” partition turns out to be wrong again, do not append another run on top.
        Invalidate the recomputed <code>success</code> instances back to <code>waiting</code> and
        replay from the corrected source again. The execution history stays; only the invalid result
        is discarded. This is the difference between reprocessing existing work and layering a
        second, contradictory result for the same business date.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="notify">
      <h2 id="notify">Consumer notification closes the loop</h2>
      <p>
        Recomputing ADS does not update a consumer that already pulled the old result. Each consumer
        needs an explicit action — reload, refresh, or a correction for a transmitted file — plus a
        confirmation that the new result is what it now holds.
      </p>
      <div class="english-table-scroll">
        <table class="english-table">
          <caption>Consumers that loaded the pre-recovery result</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Consumer</th>
              <th scope="col">State</th>
              <th scope="col">Required action</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {reference.consumerRows.map((row) => (
              <tr data-tone="wrong">
                <td>{row.label}</td>
                <td>{row.note}</td>
                <td>{row.actionLabel}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Recovery is closed only when every affected consumer has loaded the new result and confirmed
        it. A pipeline dashboard can look fully green while an external report still shows the old
        total, which is why the closure is tracked per consumer, not per pipeline.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="boundaries">
      <h2 id="boundaries">Where recovery stops and other operations begin</h2>
      <div class="english-table-scroll">
        <table class="english-table">
          <caption>Retry, rerun, backfill and recovery are different scopes</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Operation</th>
              <th scope="col">Scope</th>
              <th scope="col">Question it answers</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>
                <strong>Retry</strong>
              </td>
              <td>One attempt of the same run</td>
              <td>Was the write safe to execute again?</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                <strong>Rerun</strong>
              </td>
              <td>One existing business date</td>
              <td>How do we replace a result for a date we already processed?</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                <strong>Backfill</strong>
              </td>
              <td>A range of business dates</td>
              <td>How do we recompute history without double-writing a date?</td>
            </tr>
            <tr data-tone="right">
              <td>
                <strong>Recovery</strong>
              </td>
              <td>An impact closure after bad input</td>
              <td>
                What is affected, what must stop, what order is safe, and who still holds the old
                result?
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Retry and rerun safety depend on the write policy — see{' '}
        <a href={getEnglishRoute('idempotentEtl')}>idempotent ETL</a>. Recovery starts from the
        closure: a perfectly idempotent rerun can still be wrong if it restarts a downstream job
        before the corrected upstream result exists.
      </p>
      <p>
        Recovery also does not cover: external side effects that already left the warehouse
        (payments, emails, regulatory files), non-idempotent jobs that cannot be replayed safely,
        and cross-business-date dependencies, where a later day's rolling window must be replayed
        after the earlier correction. Those need their own checks before the replay plan is
        released.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="tradeoffs">
      <h2 id="tradeoffs">Edge cases and trade-offs</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Stop versus wait.</strong> Stopping a nearly finished aggregate wastes its work,
          but waiting for it to succeed means the bad result is published first. If publishing
          triggers consumers, stop — the wasted minutes are cheaper than the re-notification.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Blast radius versus under-recovery.</strong> Stopping the whole pipeline is simple
          and usually correct for a critical table, but it delays unrelated branches. Targeted
          recovery needs a trustworthy closure; an incomplete dependency graph turns it into
          under-recovery.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Partial replay is a trap.</strong> Restarting only the failed-looking task leaves
          already-published downstream results inconsistent. The unit of recovery is the closure,
          not the last red task.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Full rebuild is sometimes simpler.</strong> If the table is small, the dependency
          graph is unreliable, or many business dates are involved, rebuilding a range may cost less
          than maintaining a perfect closure. Decide with numbers, not habit.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Idempotency is still required.</strong> The replay plan runs instances again.
          Without an addressable target partition or merge key, the replay itself becomes another
          source of duplicates.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="english-related" aria-labelledby="related">
      <h2 id="related">Related pages</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('idempotentEtl')}>How to make an ETL job idempotent</a> — the
          write semantics that make a retry or rerun safe.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('dataQualityChecks')}>Data quality checks</a> — the gate that
          decides the corrected source is good enough to release the replay.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('lineageVsTaskDependency')}>Data lineage vs task dependency</a> —
          why the two graphs disagree, and what that does to impact analysis.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('scdType2')}>SCD Type 2 example</a> — how dimension history is
          versioned when a correction changes an attribute.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('grain')}>Data warehouse grain</a> — what one row represents, and
          why the wrong grain inflates a recomputed measure.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</EnglishArticleLayout>
